---
import AboutArticle from "../../components/AboutArticle.astro";
import ExampleFeedCard from "../../components/ExampleFeedCard.astro";
import { getAboutPage } from "../../lib/aboutPages";
import { getAboutPhoto } from "../../lib/aboutPhotos";

const page = getAboutPage("adding-devices")!;
const hero = getAboutPhoto("ethernet-cable");
---
<AboutArticle
  slug={page.slug}
  title={page.title}
  description={page.description}
  heroImage={hero.image}
  heroAlt={hero.alt}
  heroCaption={hero.caption}
  heroCredit={hero.credit}
  heroCreditHref={hero.sourceUrl}
>
  <p>
    ThermalTrace accepts readings two ways: your hardware <strong>pushes</strong> JSON to an ingest URL,
    or we <strong>pull</strong> HTTPS JSON from a public feed on a schedule. Both are configured under
    <a class="text-link" href="/dashboard/temperature">Dashboard → Devices</a>.
    New ESP / battery nodes should use push. Pull is for always-on LAN servers or relays that already expose HTTPS JSON.
  </p>
  <p>
    A free account (no credit card) ties your ingest key to your household so history and alerts stay private.
    Browse the <a class="text-link" href="/demo">live demo</a> first if you want to see readings without signing up.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose push or pull</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th></th>
          <th>Push ingest</th>
          <th>Pull feed</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Who initiates</td>
          <td>Your device</td>
          <td>ThermalTrace</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Network</td>
          <td>Device needs outbound HTTPS</td>
          <td>Feed URL must be publicly reachable</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Auth</td>
          <td>One-time per-device key in the URL</td>
          <td>Public HTTPS URL (TLS)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>JSON shape</td>
          <td>Flat keys, <code>temp</code> object, <code>sensors[]</code>, SenML, or HA REST</td>
          <td>Nested <code>temp</code> root (default); SenML or HA REST auto-detected</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Best for</td>
          <td>ESP32, Arduino, MicroPython nodes</td>
          <td>Ethernet garage servers, FastAPI relays</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Push device (recommended)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Sign in and open <a class="text-link" href="/dashboard/temperature">Devices</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Under <strong>Push devices</strong>, enter a name and click <strong>Create push device</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Copy the one-time ingest URL / key immediately (QR is on the same card). You will not see the full key again—rotate if you lose it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Map sensors so JSON keys become names on Home:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Quick add: temperature + humidity pair</strong> — same JSON key for both (e.g. <code>0</code> or <code>temp1</code>), usual DHT-style probe.
        </li>
        <li>
          Or <strong>Add one sensor</strong> for doors, floods, CO₂, etc. Pick a type; units default from the type. Calibration lives under <strong>Advanced</strong> on each sensor.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      Point firmware at <code>POST /api/ingest/&lt;key&gt;</code> with JSON that matches those keys. Sample sketches:
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/doodersrage/thermaltrace/tree/main/sketches" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">sketches/</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Smoke-test with curl, then open <a class="text-link" href="/">Home</a> while signed in. Within about 30 minutes without a post, Home warns that a probe may be offline.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Example curl (push)</h3>
  <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
    Fetch a live sample body from the
    <a class="text-link" href="/api/feeds/example?format=ingest">example ingest feed</a>
    (weather-driven), then POST it to your device key.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`curl -X POST "https://thermaltrace.dev/api/ingest/YOUR_DEVICE_KEY" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"temp1": 42.5, "door1": false, "battery": 87, "rssi": -62}'`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Payload styles — flat keys, classic <code>temp</code> object, typed <code>sensors[]</code>,
    <strong>SenML (RFC 8428)</strong>, or <strong>Home Assistant REST</strong> <code>state</code> JSON — are documented in
    <a class="text-link" href="/about/ingest-and-webhooks">ingest &amp; webhooks</a> and the developer
    <a class="text-link" href="https://doodersrage.github.io/thermaltrace/ingest/">push ingest</a> reference.
    Try live samples: <a class="text-link" href="/api/feeds/example?format=ingest">native</a>,
    <a class="text-link" href="/api/feeds/example?format=senml">SenML</a>,
    <a class="text-link" href="/api/feeds/example?format=homeassistant">HA REST</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Pull feed</h2>
  <ExampleFeedCard class="mb-6" />
  <ol>
    <li>
      Confirm your feed returns HTTPS JSON. Classic shape (default root key <code>temp</code>):
    </li>
  </ol>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "temp": {
    "0": { "c": 18.5, "f": 65.3, "h": 42 },
    "1": { "c": 19.0, "f": 66.2, "h": 40 },
    "avg": { "f": 65.75, "c": 18.75, "h": 41 }
  },
  "battery_pct": 87,
  "rssi": -62
}`}</code></pre>
  <ol start="2">
    <li>
      On <a class="text-link" href="/dashboard/temperature#pull-feeds">Devices → Pull JSON feeds</a>, expand
      <strong>Edit pull feeds</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Add a feed: name, HTTPS URL, and <strong>JSON root key</strong> (default <code>temp</code>).
      If your object lives under <code>readings</code> instead of <code>temp</code>, set the root key to <code>readings</code>.
      Use <strong>Test feed URL</strong> before saving.
    </li>
    <li>
      Under <strong>Probe labels</strong>, click <strong>Discover from feed</strong> to import JSON keys with live readings, rename them for Home, then save.
      You can also map keys manually if you prefer.
    </li>
    <li>
      Save feeds, then save probes. Wait for the next poll (or refresh Home while signed in) and confirm cards update.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Pull feeds expect nested probe objects under the configured root by default (not flat push-style keys).
    <strong>SenML JSON arrays</strong> and <strong>Home Assistant REST</strong> responses are auto-detected when that root is missing — paste the URL as-is and map the probe keys SenML or HA expose (e.g. <code>0</code>, <code>state</code>).
    If the MCU cannot do TLS, use a local HTTPS relay (see
    <a class="text-link" href="/about/python-feeds">Python feeds</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="mqtt-bridge">Already on MQTT? Bridge it</h2>
  <p>
    ThermalTrace is not an MQTT broker — keep Mosquitto or Home Assistant MQTT on your LAN.
    Mirror readings to cloud alerts and history with the HTTPS bridge:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`curl -X POST "https://thermaltrace.dev/api/ingest/mqtt" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -H "X-Ingest-Key: YOUR_DEVICE_KEY" \\
  -d '{"topic":"home/garage/temp","message":{"temp1":42.5,"humidity":38}}'`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Create a push device, map the same JSON keys as your MQTT payload, then point a bridge at
    <code>POST /api/ingest/mqtt</code> with header <code>X-Ingest-Key</code>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="home-assistant">Home Assistant</h3>
  <p>
    Prefer automatic entities? Install the official
    <a class="text-link" href="/integrations/home-assistant">HACS integration</a>
    with a Pro share-link token. For MQTT-only setups, trigger on your topic and POST via <code>rest_command</code>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`# configuration.yaml
rest_command:
  thermaltrace_ingest:
    url: "https://thermaltrace.dev/api/ingest/mqtt"
    method: POST
    headers:
      Content-Type: application/json
      X-Ingest-Key: !secret thermaltrace_ingest_key
    payload: >
      {"topic":"{{ trigger.topic }}","payload":{{ trigger.payload }}}`}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{`automation:
  - alias: ThermalTrace MQTT bridge
    trigger:
      - platform: mqtt
        topic: home/garage/temp
    action:
      - service: rest_command.thermaltrace_ingest`}</code></pre>

  <h3>Node-RED</h3>
  <p>
    Import the ready flow
    <a class="text-link" href="/nodered/mqtt-to-thermaltrace.json" download>mqtt-to-thermaltrace.json</a>,
    set your Mosquitto broker, set env <code>THERMALTRACE_INGEST_KEY</code> (or edit the function node),
    and deploy. Full write-up:
    <a class="text-link" href="https://doodersrage.github.io/thermaltrace/integrations/mqtt-bridge">developer MQTT bridge</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Dual-run is the usual pattern: local automations stay on MQTT; ThermalTrace handles household freeze SMS/email and history
    without exposing the broker. See also
    <a class="text-link" href="/compare/diy-mqtt">ThermalTrace vs DIY MQTT</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Verify you’re live</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Home</strong> — live cards update; lagging probes show a yellow offline warning after ~30 minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>Devices → Device health</strong> — last seen, battery, and RSSI when the payload includes them.</li>
    <li><strong>Overview checklist</strong> — map keys and send a test alert before you trust a freeze night.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/about/ingest-and-webhooks">Ingest API and alert webhooks</a></li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/about/kit-qr-onboarding">Kit QR onboarding</a></li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/about/configuring-temperature-feeds">Configuring temperature feeds</a> (pull deep-dive)</li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/about/debugging-stale-readings">Debugging stale readings</a></li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/compare/diy-mqtt">vs DIY MQTT</a> ·
      <a class="text-link" href="https://doodersrage.github.io/thermaltrace/integrations/mqtt-bridge">MQTT bridge docs</a></li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="https://doodersrage.github.io/thermaltrace/ingest/">Developer: push ingest</a> ·
      <a class="text-link" href="https://doodersrage.github.io/thermaltrace/ingest/pull-feeds">pull feeds</a></li>
  </ul>
</AboutArticle>
